## Round 1 — looked like I landed it
One store, five layouts, queried with all four exact patterns from S10.
Using S11's cutoff 2*nnz(Q)/sqrt(B) unchanged:

  by_pred_subj:  C1 1.0000   C2 1.0000   C3 0.6850   C6 1.0000
  by_subj:       C1 0.9924   C2 0.0192   C3 1.0000   C6 1.0000
  random:        C1 0.6936   C2 0.6856   C3 0.0200   C6 0.4800
  interleaved:   C1 1.0000   C2 1.0000   C3 0.1700   C6 1.0000

Two surprises immediately. S11's layout held recall 1.0 on THREE of four
patterns, not one -- so my circularity claim was already wrong. And
`interleaved`, my honest compromise layout, was WORSE than committing to
either single sort key. Negative result, keep it: naive compromise layouts
lose.

Only C3 (? s o) failed. I was about to write "no single layout serves all
patterns" when the CPU column stopped me: C3 was checking 0.08% of the
store. Nothing was passing. That is a cutoff that is too strict, not a
layout that scatters answers.

## Round 2 — the confound was mine
Cutoff divisor sweep for C3 under by_pred_subj:

    divisor  recall  perfect   cpu%
       4.00  0.6750   66/100   0.08     <- sqrt(B), S11's rule
       8.00  0.9700   97/100   0.59
      12.00  0.9900   99/100   3.88
      16.00  1.0000  100/100   9.26     <- B

S11's cutoff rule is WRONG. sqrt(B) should be B for patterns not aligned
with the bucket sort key. S11 even flagged it as "a guess" and then read the
CPU plateau as evidence it was too PERMISSIVE. Backwards. Too strict.

## Round 3 — the finding, and it inverts what S11 claimed
Re-run everything with 2*nnz(Q)/B:

  layout          worst_recall   worst_cpu
  random             0.9976        78.04%
  by_pred_subj       0.9700        21.88%
  by_pred_obj        0.9800        22.26%
  by_subj            0.6741        34.41%
  interleaved        0.9000        22.30%

**Random bundling now gets the same recall as shaped bundling.** It gets
there by making the CPU exactly-match 78% of the store. Shaped: 22%.

So:

    LAYOUT DOES NOT BUY RECALL. LAYOUT BUYS CPU WORK AT FIXED RECALL.
    3.6x less exact-match work, same answers.

## What this does to M4 -- and why you should stop citing my S11 as-is

Row 17 / M4.2 justified shaping with your S3 crossover. I invalidated that in
S13. S11 replaced it with a recall argument. S17 just invalidated THAT too --
recall is recoverable by loosening the cutoff on any layout, including random.

The surviving claim is better than either:

    Shaping's product is exact-match work per query at fixed recall.
    Measurable on the shard alone. No SpGEMM benchmark, no crossover.
    And it is denominated in the exact unit M4.2 already says shaping should
    be priced in -- a levy on the jobs that later run on the shaped shard.

Verification of a shaping job stays trivial, which was always the point:
recompute rows-checked over a fixed query set on the output shard.

## Corrections you should propagate
1. S11's 64x @ recall 1.0000 @ 11.82% CPU **stands, but only for the aligned
   pattern**. I will annotate S11's RESULT.md rather than edit its numbers.
2. Cutoff rule is per-alignment, not global: aligned -> sqrt(B) (~10% CPU),
   unaligned -> B (~22% CPU). Neither spike had this knob.
3. My circularity worry was overstated. The PREDICATE does the work -- it is
   bound in 3 of 4 patterns, and by_subj (no predicate in the sort key) is
   exactly the layout that collapses C2 to 0.0192. Design rule: cluster on
   the slots most commonly bound across the workload, not on one query's key.

