## HOW FAR TO A WORLD COMPUTER — the honest distance

Proven, on silicon, by someone other than the person who claimed it:
  * MeTTa evaluates on an Android phone and agrees with a desktop byte for
    byte, in interpreter order, to the fuel step. Fuel exhaustion is an
    agreeable, payable outcome. (S15)
  * MORK cross-compiles and runs there too; 33 corpus programs agree across
    macOS/Apple Silicon and Android/Snapdragon. (S16)
  * The central bet -- determinism makes verification a byte comparison --
    is the one thing here that is demonstrated rather than argued.

Not built, at all:
  * no app, no JNI, no WorkManager, no transport, no shard store, no CID, no
    market, no bid, no settlement, no attestation, no NPU code -- M3 and M4
    are zero lines, M2 is zero lines.
  * the fleet is one phone on a USB cable. adb is not a network. Node count
    of the world computer, today: 0.
  * a phone cannot be a DAS peer at all (S8: the bus dials back).
  * our own schema still lacks the worker-bound commit/reveal seal, without
    which replication proves nothing.
  * MORK -- fastest engine, fuel counter, differential harness -- is still
    unlicensed. M0.1 is one email and it is still unsent.

M1 is the only milestone with real progress, and its remaining half is
packaging rather than risk -- the DEVICE_ADDENDUM says so and I agree. M2 is
gated on one unrun measurement (QNN requantisation + can the scale be pinned).
Everything after that is unstarted.

Fair summary: this is an unusually well-evidenced feasibility study with two
genuine hardware proofs, at day one of version control. It is not a network
and does not claim to be. Its strongest asset is not any result -- it is that
six verification spikes found three of its own headline numbers wrong, and a
seventh (S17) demolished its author's own best result and published the
demolition. Keep doing that and the numbers will be worth something. The
incident above is what happens the one time you don't.

