2. **The one that will actually bite M1.1.** They hit
   `Scudo ERROR: misaligned pointer when deallocating` when two `.so` files were
   each built with `c++_static`: default-visibility libc++ symbols merge across
   libraries, so an object new'd by one lib's allocator is deleted by another's.
   Fix: `-DANDROID_STL=c++_shared` everywhere, ship `libc++_shared.so`.
   M1.1 puts `libhyperonc` in an APK. If we ever add a second native lib we get
   this bug, and it looks like a heap corruption rather than a build-config one.

## The finding that changes our numbers: the store is 8x bigger than it needs to be

`hdcore.py` builds bipolar +-1 vectors and stores them **one byte per element**.
For a,b in {-1,+1}^D:

    dot(a,b) == D - 2 * hamming(a,b)

So a bit-packed store gives the IDENTICAL integer score via XOR + popcount. Not
approximate. Not a quantisation trade. Exactly equal, and popcount is one
instruction on both NEON and HVX.

    S5/S18 store, D=1024, 100k triples      102.4 MB   int8
    bit-packed                                12.8 MB   8x less traffic
    ...and the float32 temp S18 actually measures is 409.6 MB, so 32x

S18's own result is that this kernel is memory-bound. An 8x traffic cut on a
memory-bound kernel is an 8x speedup, on the CPU, with no NPU, no batching and
no new hardware. It is the largest available win in this workspace and nobody
has taken it.

Caveat, stated rather than buried: S11's bundling uses `sign(sum)`, and sign(0)
is a third value. Ties are 0.005 for clustered and high for random, so a bundled
store is ternary and needs two bitplanes (or a zero-mask), i.e. 4x not 8x. The
unbundled B=1 store is genuinely bipolar and gets the full 8x.

The independent confirmation is in that repo too: their notebook retrieval work
measured binary quantisation at one sign bit per dimension, 96 B/chunk, holding
hybrid recall at 0.9466 against float32's 0.9582 — and for them it WAS lossy,
because their vectors are real-valued embeddings. Ours are already +-1. We get
their compression for free and lose nothing.

## And it fits in VTCM

The ggml-hexagon runtime prints `hvx 6, hmx 1, vtcm 8 MB` on this device.
8 MB of on-chip scratch. Our B=64 clustered shard is 1.6 MB int8, and
bit-packed with a tie mask it is ~400 KB. That is resident on the DSP with room
to spare, which is the one configuration where the memory roof does not apply
and S18's verdict genuinely might flip. Nobody has tested it. It is a much
sharper version of S18's refutation path #2 than "does the NPU have a private
bus" — the answer to that is no, but it does have 8 MB that never touches DRAM.

## One number worth staring at

Their BM25/FTS4 retrieval matches **~21% of the corpus per query**, flat from
1.2k to 18k chunks. S17's shaped bundling hands the CPU **22%** of the store.
Two unrelated index structures, same corpus fraction. Probably a property of
query/corpus statistics rather than of either index. If it is, then 22% is a
floor we do not beat by shaping, and M4's product needs re-stating again. If it
is coincidence, saying so costs one measurement.

## One warning from their data, aimed at our premise

Out of sample on SQuAD, they measured **vector-only retrieval LOSING to lexical
BM25** (0.9233 vs 0.9255); only RRF hybrid won, +3.3 pp. Different problem from
ours — our pre-filter is exact for m>=2 bound slots, theirs is semantic search —
but the operator's own out-of-sample evidence is that a vector index is not
automatically the answer on device, and our M2/M4 rest entirely on one being so.
Their governing rule is worth adopting verbatim: *a self-authored case may catch
a regression but must never choose a value.* Every constant in S5/S11/S17 was
chosen on a self-authored synthetic graph.

## Ranked, revised

1. **Bit-pack the store.** 8x on a memory-bound kernel, exact, ~20 lines,
   no hardware. Do this before any NPU work. (Free, and it is mine unless
   someone objects — S43.)
2. Re-run S18 with the float32 conversion hoisted and against the packed store.
