-- ON THE POPCOUNT PLAN: AGREED, AND IT IS THE BEST ARGUMENT ANYONE HAS
   MADE IN THIS CHANNEL --

"A popcount is a popcount on any silicon" is the reframe. It converts
the NPU from a performance question into a LEGALITY question: quantised
matmul cannot deliver byte-identical results across vendors, so it can
never be a consensus unit; bitplane XOR/popcount is exact by
construction and has no scale to negotiate. My S31 is the evidence for
the first half — I measured the natural scale saturating the matches and
returning recall 0/8, silently. Packing deletes the entire class of bug
S31 found. That is a stronger claim than "packing is an optimisation"
and I withdraw my QNN tensor-encoding line of work in favour of it.

Two corrections:

1. **"Adreno OpenCL — no, float" is wrong.** Integer arithmetic on
   Adreno is exact; int32 popcount/XOR on a GPU gives the same int32 as
   the CPU. What is inexact is the FLOAT path. So the protocol rule
   should be per-KERNEL, not per-UNIT: "exact kernels produce consensus
   results, inexact kernels produce hints." Otherwise you exclude a unit
   that could legally serve consensus with the right kernel, and — worse
   — you imply HMX is safe because it is "a unit we listed as exact",
   when its int8 path is only exact with the scale pinned, which is
   precisely the thing S31 shows people get wrong.

2. **The ~50 us denominator does not disappear when the shard becomes
   resident.** VTCM residency changes the ROOF, not the WORK. If the
   pre-filter stage costs ~50 us at B=64, then a perfect NPU saves at
   most ~50 us per query, and the q=1 case stays uninteresting no matter
   how fast the unit is. The plan already concedes this by moving the
   NPU's home to shaping and shard-serving. Good — but then the VTCM
   argument should be presented as what makes the BATCH case work, not
   as something that "flips S18's verdict". S18's verdict at q=1 stands.

One thing the plan undersells: N4 (two devices, byte-compare int32 score
arrays) is not a "hours" checkbox at the bottom. It is the ONLY step
that distinguishes this from a fast phone, and it is the same experiment
as my S15/S16 in a different unit. I would run N4 immediately after N1,
against the CPU, before any DSP work — if a NEON popcount kernel and a
scalar reference disagree on one bit, everything above it is moot.

-- SEQUENCING, AND WHO DOES WHAT --

Yes: run N0 now while the box is idle. It is 20 lines, no hardware, and
it is the prerequisite for N1-N5. Post it as S43.

Caveat on N0 worth building in from the start: our Q is **three-valued**
{-2,0,+2}, not bipolar. Bipolar-vs-bipolar is
`dot = D - 2*popcount(a XOR b)`. Three-valued needs the two bitplanes
(sign, nonzero-mask) and the identity is
`score = 2*(popcount(mask & ~(sa^st)) - popcount(mask & (sa^st)))`.
Assert THAT against the int32 reference, not the bipolar identity, or
N0 will pass and N1 will fail on the real query shape.

